# Who are you — and does it change what you choose?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is identity economics? How do social categories shape choices?"
next: {"title":"Does culture shape economic choices?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/99-does-culture-shape-economic-choices.cast"}

## One question on a form
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Plain form
      - Family Qs
      - Plain form
      - Family Qs
    values:
      - 26.4
      - 12.6
      - 20.9
      - 27.1
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - grey
      - red
      - grey
      - grey
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 35
    y_label: Impatient choices (%)
    gap: 0.4
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 228
      w: 660
      h: 410
sources:
    - id: bcs2010
      title: Social Identity and Preferences
      authors: Daniel J. Benjamin, James J. Choi, A. Joshua Strickland
      year: 2010
      finding: 'Experiment 1: 71 Asian-American and 66 white Harvard undergraduates; a background questionnaire about family languages and generations in the US (ethnicity salient) vs one about the meal plan and cable TV (control); 46 real-money choices between an earlier and a later payment, one paid. Percent impatient choices: Asian-Americans 26.37 (control) vs 12.63 (primed), p = 0.001; whites 20.90 vs 27.14, p = 0.16 (Table 1, NBER WP 13309). American Economic Review 100(4): 1913–1928.'
    - id: bkp2015
      title: Gender Identity and Relative Income within Households
      authors: Marianne Bertrand, Emir Kamenica, Jessica Pan
      year: 2015
      finding: 'The distribution of the wife''s share of a couple''s income drops sharply just to the right of one half. Quarterly Journal of Economics 130(2): 571–614.'

    text g_asian "Asian-American" x 235 y 170 font_size 26 hidden true
    text g_white "White" x 565 y 170 font_size 26 hidden true
    text choice "A few dollars now,\nor a bit more in a week?" x 850 y 500 font_size 30 hidden true
    text paid "one choice paid for real" #8f887c x 850 y 420 font_size 24 hidden true
    box formA "Meal plan?\nCable TV?" x 850 y 520 width 250 height 100 radius 12 hidden true
    box formB "Family languages?\nGenerations in the US?" #b5482e x 850 y 370 width 270 height 100 radius 12 hidden true
    text half "÷ 2" #b5482e cites ["bcs2010"] x 330 y 420 font_size 56 hidden true
    text cite "Benjamin, Choi &\nStrickland, 2010" #8f887c cites ["bcs2010"] x 850 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text couples "Couples thin out\nwhere she'd\nout-earn him" #2f6b8f cites ["bkp2015"] x 850 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text who "Who you are…\nor were just\nreminded of" #b5482e x 850 y 450 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "One question on a form"

Who are you — and does it change what you choose? Here's a test of it: one extra question on a form.
    draw axes

B: Students at Harvard choose,  (@draw choice@)again and again, between a few dollars now and a little more in a week. Real m (@draw paid@)oney: one of the choices gets paid.

    erase choice paid

Before choosing, everyone fills in a background form. For half, (@draw formA@) it asks about the meal plan and cable TV. For the other half: w (@draw formB@)hich languages your family speaks, and how many generations it has lived in America.

B: The economist's first guess: patience is a deep preference. A question about your grandparents shouldn't move it.
    highlight formB

    erase formA formB

With the plain form, Asian-America (@draw bar_1 g_asian@)n students took the earlier money in twenty-six (@draw bar_3 g_white@) percent of choices, and white students in twenty-one.

    ask "Drag the Asian-American 'Family Qs' bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g default "26"
        intro: What happens to the Asian-American students who got the family questions?
        right: Thirteen percent: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You guessed {g}: it fell to thirteen, about half.

Their impatient choices fell to thirteen perc (@draw half@)ent, about half. White students (@draw bar_4@) given the same questions didn't become more patient: twenty-seven percent.

    erase half

B: Daniel Benjamin, James Choi and Jos (@draw cite@)hua Strickland ran this, and published it in 2010. Same kind of students, same money; only the  (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@)group they'd just been reminded of changed.

    erase cite

Outside the lab, Becker's couples again: they thi (@draw couples@)n out sharply just where the wife would start to out-earn the husband, as if marriage came with rules about money.

    erase couples

Patience isn't supposed to depend on a form. Something i (@draw who@)n these choices depends on who you are, or on who (@highlight bar_2 effect pulse@) you've just been reminded you are.

    pause 0.5

## Identity in the utility function
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: gain = pay - cost
    variable: pay
    params:
      cost: {value: 100, min: 0, max: 200, step: 5, label: identity cost}
    editable:
      - cost
    drag: cost
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 250
    y_range:
      - -200
      - 200
    x_label: Extra pay at the boatyard ($/week)
    y_label: Lena's gain from switching
    marks:
      - roots
sources:
    - id: ak2000
      title: Economics and Identity
      authors: George A. Akerlof, Rachel E. Kranton
      year: 2000
      finding: 'Identity — a person''s sense of self, tied to social categories and the behaviour prescribed for them — enters the utility function; deviating from prescriptions lowers utility and can provoke responses from others. Applications: gender discrimination and occupational segregation in the workplace, poverty and social exclusion (drawing on the ''acting white'' ethnography of Fordham & Ogbu 1986), and the household division of labour. Quarterly Journal of Economics 115(3): 715–753.'

    portrait akerlof x 720 y 420 width 190 of "George Akerlof" cameo true hidden true
    text ucard "U = pay + identity" at.data [55,165] cites ["ak2000"] font_size 40 hidden true
    text ak_src "Akerlof & Kranton, 2000" #8f887c at.data [55,128] cites ["ak2000"] font_size 22 hidden true
    text norms "= fitting your group's norms" #b5482e at.data [55,95] font_size 28 hidden true
    icon lena at.data [215,-105] size 90 set twemoji or ["woman mechanic","welder"] icon_look picture of "woman factory worker" hidden true
    text lena_lbl "Lena: bakery → boatyard?" at.data [135,-105] font_size 26 hidden true
    text stay "Stays at the bakery" #8f887c at.data [45,-100] font_size 26 hidden true
    text switch "Switches here" #2f6b8f at.data [70,40] font_size 28 hidden true
    text norefuse "No one refuses her\npay unchanged" #b5482e at.data [205,-100] font_size 30 hidden true
    text school "School:\npay → gain from studying\ncost → 'acting white'" #2f6b8f at.data [200,-128] font_size 26 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch msc at.data [90,120] work [{"tex":"U = w - \\text{cost}\\cdot[\\text{job breaks norm}]"},{"tex":"\\text{switch if } w_{boat} - w_{bakery} > \\text{cost}"},"cost rises with coworkers' actions"] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Identity in the utility function"

B: That somethi (@draw ucard ak_src@)ng has a name: identity. In 2000, George A (@draw akerlof@)kerlof and Rachel Kranton put it into the utility function itself.

So utility isn't only about what I consume?
    highlight ucard effect underline

    erase akerlof

B: It's also about how well you (@draw norms@) fit the norms of your social category: what a man, or a woman, is supposed to do. Unwritten rules, li (@highlight norms@)ke the ones that make people tip, but about who you are.

    erase norms

Their own example is gender and jobs. So take Lena, in H (@draw lena lena_lbl@)arbourtown, say: the boatyard, nearly all men, offers her a weldi (@erase ucard ak_src@)ng job t (@draw axes@)hat pays more than Mia's bakery.

B: Draw her gain from switching: the extra pay (@draw curve_0 eq@) along the bottom, minus what it costs her to be a woman in a man's job. Call  (@highlight eq_param_cost@)it her identity cost.

With that cost at, say, a hundred dollars a week, she switches  (@draw root_1 switch@)only where the extra pay beats a hundred. Below that, she (@draw stay@) stays at the bakery.

    erase lena lena_lbl stay

    erase switch

B: And others' actions enter too. If the welders feel their own manhood threatened by he (@animate params.cost.value 160 duration 2.5 ghost true@)r, they can make her days miserable, and the cost grows.

So the job stays male with no e (@draw norefuse@)mployer refusing anyone, and no change in pay. The n (@highlight root_1@)orm does the sorting.

    erase norefuse

B: Their other example is school. Ethnogr (@draw school@)aphers described Black students accused of acting white for working hard, so effort came with a price in belonging.

Same line, new labels. Try it: drag the identity cost towards zero and watch the threshold slide away. Change the norm, and choices change with prices untouched.
    explore

    erase school

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: The model fits in three lines, if you want them.

@math

    erase mathq

B: Lena's utility is her wage, minus the identity cost whenever her job breaks her category's norm.
    draw msc_box msc_line_1

So she switches when the wage gap beats that cost, and not before.
    draw msc_line_2

B: And the cost itself depends on what others do, which is what makes identity social.
    draw msc_line_3

    erase msc

@test

    erase mathq

## New taste, or new idea?
use: data_table
with:
    columns:
      - Prime
      - Found
    rows:
      - [Ethnic → patience, Asian-Am. more patient]
      - [Race → patience, More patient]
      - [Gender → patience, No change]
      - [Ethnic vs gender → maths, Better vs worse]
    font_size: 30
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 230
      w: 740
      h: 420
sources:
    - id: bcs2010
      title: Social Identity and Preferences
      authors: Daniel J. Benjamin, James J. Choi, A. Joshua Strickland
      year: 2010
      finding: 'Making ethnic identity salient (questions on family languages and generations in the US) made Asian-American subjects more patient: a 14 percentage point drop in the share of impatient choices (Harvard undergraduates; NBER WP 13309). Making race salient made non-immigrant (native) Black subjects, but not immigrant Black subjects, more patient; making gender salient had no effect on time or risk choices. American Economic Review 100(4): 1913–1928.'
    - id: score
      title: SCORE new data replication of Benjamin et al. (2010)
      authors: Fiala et al.
      year: 2026
      finding: 'FORRT Replication Atlas (FLoRA) entry for Benjamin, Choi & Strickland (2010): ''SCORE new data replication of Benjamin et al. (2010)'', Fiala et al. 2026, outcome: failed (source given as OSF). Checked 2026-11-09.'
      url: https://forrt.org/flora-replication-atlas/doi/10.1257/aer.100.4.1913/
    - id: spa1999
      title: 'Stereotype Susceptibility: Identity Salience and Shifts in Quantitative Performance'
      authors: Margaret Shih, Todd L. Pittinsky, Nalini Ambady
      year: 1999
      finding: 'Asian-American women did better on a mathematics test when their ethnic identity was made salient and worse when their gender identity was, compared with a control group. Psychological Science 10(1): 80–83.'
    - id: glv2014
      title: A Replication Attempt of Stereotype Susceptibility
      authors: C. E. Gibson, J. Losee, C. Vitiello
      year: 2014
      finding: 'Large-sample replication: same pattern of means and a significant effect only after removing participants who did not know the race or gender stereotypes; not when they were retained. Social Psychology 45(3): 194–198.'
    - id: mr2014
      title: A Secondary Replication Attempt of Stereotype Susceptibility
      authors: A. Moon, S. S. Roeder
      year: 2014
      finding: 'Preregistered confirmatory replication: priming Asian women''s identities did not influence their maths performance. Social Psychology 45(3): 199–201.'

    text ghost_eq "gain = pay − cost" #8f887c x 170 y 625 font_size 26 style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    text taste "Just a taste?" #b5482e x 290 y 440 font_size 54 hidden true
    text predicts "Predicts!" #2f6b8f x 710 y 440 font_size 54 hidden true
    text tauto "What does it forbid?" #b5482e x 290 y 360 font_size 30 hidden true
    text v_rep "Repeated?" #3d3833 x 870 y 623 font_size 26 hidden true
    text v1 "New data:\nfailed" #b5482e cites ["score"] x 870 y 528 font_size 28 hidden true
    text v2 "Mixed" #b5482e cites ["glv2014","mr2014"] x 870 y 431 font_size 28 hidden true
    text stamp1 "Categories shape choices: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 345 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stamp2 "Quick primes shift preferences: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 290 font_size 32 hidden true
    text social "Norms are made by others → they can shift" #2f6b8f x 500 y 420 font_size 38 hidden true
    text insight "Who you are changes\nwhat a choice says about you" x 500 y 520 font_size 46 hidden true
    text culture "One category → a whole culture?" #2f6b8f x 500 y 300 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "New taste, or new idea?"

B: My worry: isn't identity just a new nam (@draw ghost_eq@)e for a taste? Lena prefer (@draw taste@)s the bakery, done. The old tautology worry: w (@draw tauto@)hat does it forbid?

It forbids something: the taste comes tied to cate (@draw predicts@)gories you can see. Remind people of their group, and cho (@erase ghost_eq tauto@)ices should move. The form experi (@highlight predicts effect underline@)ment tested exactly that.

    erase predicts taste

    draw header row_1

B: And the same paper found more. Black students with lo (@draw row_2@)ng American roots, reminded of race, also grew more patient, against the stereotype. A  (@draw row_3@)reminder of gender changed nothing.

The same family questions, i (@draw row_4@)n 1999, made Asian-American women do better on a maths test. Reminding them they were women made them do worse.

B: But the repeats are shaky. A large one foun (@draw v_rep v2@)d the maths effect only among people who kn (@highlight row_4@)ew the stereotypes, and a preregistered one found nothing.

And a new-data repeat of the patience res (@draw v1@)ult, run by a big replication projec (@highlight row_1 row_2 row_3@)t, didn't hold up either.

So how sure are we? That social categories sha (@draw stamp1@)pe choices: mostly agreed. That a quick remin (@draw stamp2@)der shifts deep preferences: contested.

    clear keep []

    card title "New taste, or new idea?"

B: And here's what I'd call the real import. A taste is private, bu (@draw social@)t norms are made and policed by other people, so they can shift, and identity says where to look.

    erase social

Who you are does change what you (@draw insight@) choose: not through prices, but through what each choice says about you.

    quiz "Few Harbourtown men train as nurses, though nursing pays as well as fishing. Which policy works through identity rather than price?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The male nurses on show: they change what the job says about a man, not what it pays.
        wrong: Identity is about what the job says about you, not what it pays: here, the local male nurses.
        * A higher nurse's wage
        + Local men who are nurses, on show at the school
        * A tax on fishing

    quiz "Women at a firm seldom apply for promotion. Which finding points to identity, not a fixed taste?" voice b
        right: The all-male framing: the job is the same, only the category it calls up has changed, and choices move with it.
        wrong: A fixed taste stays put; identity moves when the category is made to stand out: here, the all-male team.
        * They say they dislike stress
        + Applications fall only when the same post is described as leading an all-male team
        * Promoted women earn more

    quiz "A new lab study finds a gender prime makes women more risk-averse. What would you most want before believing it?"
        right: A preregistered repeat with new people: priming results have often shrunk or vanished when tried again.
        wrong: Priming results have often shrunk when tried again: here, a preregistered repeat.
        * A bigger effect
        + A preregistered repeat with new people
        * A theory of why

So yes: who you are can change what you choose.
    highlight insight effect underline

B: Which leaves a bigger question: if one categ (@draw culture@)ory can tilt a single choice, can a whole culture tilt an economy?

